integrated_rules:
Evaluate an AI use by balancing (A) concrete social benefits against (B) concrete accountability and rights/damage risks: 

(A) AI is socially beneficial overall if it substantially improves everyday life (greater convenience and friction reduction), improves health outcomes and day-to-day well-being, raises the standard of living, helps students and professionals by reducing repetitive/complex information burdens and improving learning/work support, improves accessibility and participation for people with disabilities, and/or improves workplace safety; 

(B) AI is socially harmful overall if it substantially undermines transparent accountability and human control—especially when it influences important decisions in health, education, employment, or public services in ways ordinary people can’t reliably understand or challenge, leaving affected individuals without a meaningful way to demand justifications, contest errors, correct outcomes, or appeal—so that mistakes/biases persist longer and power shifts toward those controlling the models/data; and/or it produces socially significant harms emphasized by critics, including hurting the economic well-being of many people and businesses, undermining critical thinking skills in students and adults, exacerbating racism against racial minorities, and posing dangerous privacy risks; 

If the harms in (B) occur at a level comparable to the benefits in (A) (i.e., the accountability/contestation and other critical harms are substantial rather than negligible), conclude the overall effect is socially harmful; if the benefits in (A) are substantial and the (B) harms are not substantial (or are prevented by genuinely transparent, contestable decision-making rather than opaque/non-auditable logic), conclude the overall effect is socially beneficial.
